WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.884
This is a technical deep dive on
Infoblox DDI for federal agencies: DNS,

2
00:00:05.884 --> 00:00:10.511
DHCP and IP address management,
delivered on Infoblox's

3
00:00:10.511 --> 00:00:15.238
NIOS Grid and on its cloud-managed
service, Universal DDI.

4
00:00:15.238 --> 00:00:19.372
It's for the architects and engineers
who will decide whether to build an

5
00:00:19.372 --> 00:00:23.438
agency's name and address layer on
Infoblox, and how to run it once they do.

6
00:00:23.438 --> 00:00:27.063
After a five-minute orientation,
it comes in two parts of about

7
00:00:27.063 --> 00:00:30.901
twenty-two minutes each, in four
modules you can watch on their own.

8
00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:33.690
Part 1 covers the platform,
then the case for it:

9
00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:38.683
which federal requirements it answers,
what its FedRAMP listing actually covers,

10
00:00:38.683 --> 00:00:43.749
how it compares with BlueCat, EfficientIP,
Microsoft and the native cloud services,

11
00:00:43.749 --> 00:00:46.613
and the risks you have to
manage if you choose it.

12
00:00:46.613 --> 00:00:50.561
Part 2 covers divisions: how
a central team hands DNS,

13
00:00:50.561 --> 00:00:56.622
DHCP and address management to bureaus
and offices, on NIOS and on Universal DDI,

14
00:00:56.622 --> 00:01:00.939
without losing control, and it
ends with a worked example,

15
00:01:00.939 --> 00:01:04.612
a responsibility matrix
and a rollout sequence.

16
00:01:04.612 --> 00:01:05.924
Two ground rules.

17
00:01:05.924 --> 00:01:11.962
Every fact here comes from a primary source
opened on October twenty-second, 2026.

18
00:01:11.962 --> 00:01:14.773
And anything that is
only Infoblox marketing

19
00:01:14.773 --> 00:01:17.213
is called a vendor claim when it appears.

20
00:01:17.213 --> 00:01:24.671
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x and
Universal DDI docs; FedRAMP Marketplace

21
00:01:24.671 --> 00:01:33.370
products.json; NIST CMVP; DISA STIG file
store; OMB and CISA publications; Book 08.

22
00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.308
Why this matters.

23
00:01:35.308 --> 00:01:39.732
Almost every connection an agency
makes starts with a DNS lookup,

24
00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:45.058
and in a hybrid, multi-cloud estate that
answer decides far more than an address.

25
00:01:45.058 --> 00:01:49.095
DHCP and IP address management
sit right beside it.

26
00:01:49.095 --> 00:01:55.408
NIST's Secure DNS Deployment Guide,
SP 800-81 Revision 3, tells agencies

27
00:01:55.408 --> 00:02:01.395
to keep DHCP lease history so an IP
address can be mapped to an asset.

28
00:02:01.395 --> 00:02:04.878
And the address plan is the
record behind everything the

29
00:02:04.878 --> 00:02:08.658
inventory directives ask for,
which we'll see on the next slide.

30
00:02:08.658 --> 00:02:11.001
That's why this deck treats DNS,

31
00:02:11.001 --> 00:02:15.969
DHCP and IPAM as one control
plane rather than three services.

32
00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:21.395
Book 08 of the workbook describes what that
plane is made of, and it isn't one box.

33
00:02:21.395 --> 00:02:26.688
There's an authoritative and recursive
layer on premises that hosts internal zones,

34
00:02:26.688 --> 00:02:30.620
answers clients, and applies
response policy zones and DNSSEC.

35
00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:35.192
There's a management plane, more and
more often delivered as a cloud service,

36
00:02:35.192 --> 00:02:37.699
that holds the system of record for zones,

37
00:02:37.699 --> 00:02:40.870
forwarding rules, address
data and security policy.

38
00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:44.207
And there are local resolvers
and agents that keep answering

39
00:02:44.207 --> 00:02:47.282
from local state if the management
plane can't be reached.

40
00:02:47.282 --> 00:02:51.708
Cloud DNS isn't replaced;
it's managed as an overlay.

41
00:02:51.708 --> 00:02:54.083
So here is the argument
of the whole session.

42
00:02:54.083 --> 00:02:57.496
Federal rules require
capabilities, not products.

43
00:02:57.496 --> 00:03:03.008
Those capabilities, taken together, need
one authoritative and governed DDI plane.

44
00:03:03.008 --> 00:03:06.750
Infoblox is one of the strongest
ways to deliver that plane,

45
00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:10.721
with evidence, and it also carries
real risks you have to manage.

46
00:03:10.721 --> 00:03:11.870
We'll cover both.

47
00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:20.670
Sources: NIST SP 800-81r3;
Book 08, Chapters 01 and 08.

48
00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:25.320
Here is what the rules actually
require, stated as capabilities.

49
00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:33.196
DNSSEC: SC-20 in the low and moderate
baselines means signing agency zones,

50
00:03:33.196 --> 00:03:37.456
and OMB M-08-23, from
2008, is still in force;

51
00:03:37.456 --> 00:03:43.395
we checked every 2025 and 2026
memo, and none rescinds it.

52
00:03:43.395 --> 00:03:48.070
SC-21 means validating answers
on recursive resolvers.

53
00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:54.445
SC-22 asks for fault-tolerant DNS with
internal and external roles separated.

54
00:03:54.445 --> 00:03:58.287
TIC 3.0, version 3.3, carries signing and

55
00:03:58.287 --> 00:04:02.495
validation as policy
enforcement point capabilities.

56
00:04:02.495 --> 00:04:09.957
Encrypted DNS: M-22-09 says agencies must
resolve DNS using encrypted DNS wherever

57
00:04:09.957 --> 00:04:15.058
it's technically supported, and
Executive Order 14144, section 4(c),

58
00:04:15.058 --> 00:04:18.553
which the June 2025
amendment left standing,

59
00:04:18.553 --> 00:04:25.070
repeats the push and adds contract language
for any product that acts as a resolver.

60
00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:28.012
CISA's Protective DNS sits upstream:

61
00:04:28.012 --> 00:04:33.232
internal resolvers forward to it,
and the agency keeps its own logs.

62
00:04:33.232 --> 00:04:34.494
Logging is subtle.

63
00:04:34.494 --> 00:04:44.395
M-26-14, from May 2026, rescinded
M-21-31 and doesn't mention DNS anywhere.

64
00:04:44.395 --> 00:04:50.480
What carries DNS and DHCP logging forward
is CISA's Logging Reference Architecture

65
00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:53.479
from August, which maps
the old DNS and DHCP

66
00:04:53.479 --> 00:04:56.907
categories into its
network-activity baseline.

67
00:04:56.907 --> 00:05:00.570
Retention is six months searchable
and twelve retrievable.

68
00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:08.847
Inventory: BOD 23-01 wants discovery of
all agency IPv4 space every seven days,

69
00:05:08.847 --> 00:05:13.245
and BOD 26-04, which
replaced 19-02 and 22-01,

70
00:05:13.245 --> 00:05:19.970
wants public IP addresses and
domains attested every quarter.

71
00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:26.070
M-21-07 set IPv6-only
targets that have all passed.

72
00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:29.895
And SC-13 wants validated cryptography.

73
00:05:29.895 --> 00:05:31.583
Now the honest starting point.

74
00:05:31.583 --> 00:05:37.858
No rule names Infoblox or any DDI vendor,
and nothing requires a DDI platform.

75
00:05:37.858 --> 00:05:44.145
Windows DNS, BIND, or cloud DNS with a
separate IPAM can meet these rules too.

76
00:05:44.145 --> 00:05:46.895
Choosing a platform is an
architecture decision.

77
00:05:46.895 --> 00:06:00.560
Sources: NIST SP 800-53r5 OSCAL 5.2.0;
OMB M-08-23, M-22-09, M-26-14, M-21-07;

78
00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:13.784
EO 14144, EO 14306; CISA Protective
DNS, Logging Reference Architecture,

79
00:06:13.784 --> 00:06:23.180
TIC 3.0 v3.3, BOD 23-01,
BOD 26-04; NIST CMVP.

80
00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:25.868
Module 1: the platform.

81
00:06:25.868 --> 00:06:28.424
Before arguing for or against Infoblox,

82
00:06:28.424 --> 00:06:31.988
we need an accurate picture of
what it is in October 2026,

83
00:06:31.988 --> 00:06:36.481
because the names have changed and a
lot of older material is now wrong.

84
00:06:36.481 --> 00:06:41.724
We'll start with the NIOS Grid, the
on-premises architecture most agencies know,

85
00:06:41.724 --> 00:06:46.160
then the cloud-managed side,
Universal DDI and the Infoblox Portal,

86
00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:49.468
including the limits
Infoblox itself documents.

87
00:06:49.468 --> 00:06:54.356
Then DNS, DHCP and IPAM in turn,
the Threat Defense security layer,

88
00:06:54.356 --> 00:06:59.867
and finally the automation interfaces
and the logging and reporting path.

89
00:06:59.867 --> 00:07:03.719
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x admin guide;

90
00:07:03.719 --> 00:07:09.010
Universal DDI and Threat
Defense documentation.

91
00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:14.517
The NIOS Grid is a group of appliances
that share sections of one distributed

92
00:07:14.517 --> 00:07:19.572
database, configured and monitored through
a single access point, the Grid Master.

93
00:07:19.572 --> 00:07:24.007
The Grid Master can be one appliance
or a high-availability pair, and every

94
00:07:24.007 --> 00:07:26.550
administrator reaches the GUI through it,

95
00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:29.385
even administrators scoped
to a single member.

96
00:07:29.385 --> 00:07:35.860
Members can be physical Infoblox hardware
or virtual vNIOS, singly or in HA pairs.

97
00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:41.878
The Grid Master talks to every member
hub-and-spoke, over encrypted VPN tunnels

98
00:07:41.878 --> 00:07:47.897
on UDP 1194 by default, after a
challenge-response authentication on port 2114.

99
00:07:47.897 --> 00:07:51.898
A Grid Master Candidate holds a
complete copy of the database.

100
00:07:51.898 --> 00:07:56.236
Promoting it is a manual operation, and
you can't do it from the cloud portal.

101
00:07:56.236 --> 00:07:59.738
HA pairs share one virtual
IP and virtual MAC,

102
00:07:59.738 --> 00:08:03.147
with VRRP advertisements as the heartbeat.

103
00:08:03.147 --> 00:08:08.948
A Grid can run IPv4-only,
IPv6-only or dual mode.

104
00:08:08.948 --> 00:08:10.135
Form factors.

105
00:08:10.135 --> 00:08:16.360
The current hardware line is Trinzic
X6, supported from NIOS 9.0.1.

106
00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:21.491
vNIOS for VMware runs from four virtual
CPUs and thirty-two gigabytes up to

107
00:08:21.491 --> 00:08:27.035
thirty-two CPUs and three hundred eighty-four,
and every model can be a Grid Master.

108
00:08:27.035 --> 00:08:33.088
vNIOS for Azure supports HA from 9.0.4
and explicitly covers Azure Government;

109
00:08:33.088 --> 00:08:39.235
note that Azure doesn't support running
DHCP in Azure for on-premises networks.

110
00:08:39.235 --> 00:08:45.735
vNIOS for AWS can be a Grid Master in HA,
and its vDiscovery has a GovCloud option.

111
00:08:45.735 --> 00:08:51.573
Google Cloud and Oracle Cloud are
covered too, with OCI HA new in 9.1.

112
00:08:51.573 --> 00:08:56.572
NIOS-X servers are the data plane
for Universal DDI, which is next.

113
00:08:56.572 --> 00:09:04.222
Versions: NIOS 9.1.0 went GA in
March 2026 and ships BIND 9.20.

114
00:09:04.222 --> 00:09:07.314
NIOS 9.0.6 introduced a Long-Term Support

115
00:09:07.314 --> 00:09:10.873
train with up to twenty-four
months of support.

116
00:09:10.873 --> 00:09:18.562
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x About Grids,
Promoting a Grid Master Candidate,

117
00:09:18.562 --> 00:09:24.109
About HA Failover, 9.1.0 What's
New; vNIOS install guides;

118
00:09:24.109 --> 00:09:31.900
Supported Platforms for NIOS-X Servers;
Infoblox NIOS 9.0.6 and 9.1.0 blogs.

119
00:09:32.650 --> 00:09:35.788
Universal DDI is the cloud-managed side.

120
00:09:35.788 --> 00:09:41.461
It used to be called BloxOne DDI;
Infoblox renamed it in September 2024,

121
00:09:41.461 --> 00:09:45.276
and the on-premises hosts
became NIOS-X servers.

122
00:09:45.276 --> 00:09:51.329
The old Cloud Services Portal is now the
Infoblox Portal, although the documentation

123
00:09:51.329 --> 00:09:56.275
still uses both names and the API host
is still csp dot infoblox dot com.

124
00:09:56.275 --> 00:10:00.924
The data plane is NIOS-X servers that
you run, or NIOS-X as a Service,

125
00:10:00.924 --> 00:10:06.363
which Infoblox runs from its own points
of presence in AWS and Google Cloud.

126
00:10:06.363 --> 00:10:11.610
The management plane reaches
outward to Route 53, Azure DNS,

127
00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:17.713
Google Cloud DNS, Cloudflare and
Akamai, and to Microsoft DNS and DHCP.

128
00:10:17.713 --> 00:10:21.494
It can also manage NIOS
Grids from inside the Portal,

129
00:10:21.494 --> 00:10:27.212
and the documented hybrid DNS pattern
makes NIOS primary and NIOS-X secondary.

130
00:10:27.212 --> 00:10:31.605
Infoblox markets this as a single
cloud-based control plane and API;

131
00:10:31.605 --> 00:10:36.312
that's a vendor claim, and the limits on
the right are why you should test it.

132
00:10:36.312 --> 00:10:39.312
These limits come from
Infoblox's own pages.

133
00:10:39.312 --> 00:10:44.273
Inside the Portal, the embedded NIOS view
can't promote a Grid Master Candidate,

134
00:10:44.273 --> 00:10:49.538
show Splunk reporting or download files,
and Multi-Grid Management isn't supported.

135
00:10:49.538 --> 00:10:54.301
NIOS DHCP failover associations
can't be managed from the Portal.

136
00:10:54.301 --> 00:10:57.375
WAPI Passthrough reaches
only the Grid Master.

137
00:10:57.375 --> 00:11:02.338
NIOS-X as a Service has no IPv6,
no overlapping IP spaces and no

138
00:11:02.338 --> 00:11:07.500
Azure points of presence, which
matters under the IPv6 mandate.

139
00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:12.939
The cloud DNS integrations sync simple
routing records only, without tags,

140
00:11:12.939 --> 00:11:17.500
with NS records read-only and
alias records turned into CNAMEs.

141
00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:20.212
And Google's DNSSEC records don't sync.

142
00:11:20.212 --> 00:11:22.264
So "one console" is overstated:

143
00:11:22.264 --> 00:11:26.624
in practice you'll run Grid Manager
and the Portal side by side.

144
00:11:26.624 --> 00:11:33.522
Sources: Infoblox What's New in Universal
DDI; Limitations of Universal DDI;

145
00:11:33.522 --> 00:11:40.975
Limitations of NIOS-X as a Service; Universal
DDI and NIOS Grid Hybrid Deployment;

146
00:11:40.975 --> 00:11:45.091
Route 53, Azure DNS and
GCP integration pages;

147
00:11:45.091 --> 00:11:49.120
infoblox.com Universal DDI page (vendor).

148
00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:54.795
DNS on NIOS 9.1, with the
catch for each feature.

149
00:11:54.795 --> 00:11:58.313
DNS views serve different
answers to different clients

150
00:11:58.313 --> 00:12:01.607
from match lists of client
or destination addresses.

151
00:12:01.607 --> 00:12:05.137
The first match wins, and you
can set forwarders per view,

152
00:12:05.137 --> 00:12:09.282
which is how you'd point an internal
view at CISA's Protective DNS.

153
00:12:09.282 --> 00:12:12.769
DNSSEC is strong, with
structure you must plan around.

154
00:12:12.769 --> 00:12:15.958
Only the Grid Master can be
primary for a signed zone.

155
00:12:15.958 --> 00:12:20.444
It acts as a hidden primary and
members serve the zone as secondaries.

156
00:12:20.444 --> 00:12:25.460
Keys live either on the Grid Master
or on an external network HSM,

157
00:12:25.460 --> 00:12:28.582
Thales Luna or Entrust nShield, not both.

158
00:12:28.582 --> 00:12:32.993
Zone-signing keys roll automatically
every thirty days by default,

159
00:12:32.993 --> 00:12:36.458
and key-signing keys yearly,
automatically or by hand.

160
00:12:36.458 --> 00:12:41.663
If the parent zone is in the Grid, the new
DS record is inserted for you; otherwise

161
00:12:41.663 --> 00:12:46.708
someone exports it to the parent, which
for a dot-gov zone means the registrar.

162
00:12:46.708 --> 00:12:51.808
DNS Firewall uses response policy
zones: local, feed or FireEye,

163
00:12:51.808 --> 00:12:56.908
up to thirty-two of them, with hits
logged to syslog in CEF format.

164
00:12:56.908 --> 00:13:01.858
GSS-TSIG lets Windows domain controllers
make Kerberos-signed updates,

165
00:13:01.858 --> 00:13:04.746
up to two hundred
fifty-six keys per member.

166
00:13:04.746 --> 00:13:10.745
Grid members can manage Windows DNS
and DHCP servers without an agent,

167
00:13:10.745 --> 00:13:16.020
over MS-RPC, read-only or read-write,
and 9.1 adds IPv6 management.

168
00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:21.632
Encrypted DNS: from 9.1.0,
DNS over TLS and DNS over

169
00:13:21.632 --> 00:13:27.532
HTTPS are served by BIND,
but only on X6 appliances.

170
00:13:27.532 --> 00:13:32.145
Infoblox says they're free on X6
and need an extra license on X5.

171
00:13:32.145 --> 00:13:35.224
One configuration item you must change:

172
00:13:35.224 --> 00:13:38.770
the default DNSSEC
algorithm is RSA with SHA-1.

173
00:13:38.770 --> 00:13:43.995
Pick SHA-256 or an ECDSA curve
before you sign anything.

174
00:13:43.995 --> 00:13:51.932
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x: Configuring
DNS Views, Anycast, DNSSEC on a Grid,

175
00:13:51.932 --> 00:13:59.999
Setting DNSSEC Parameters, Thales Luna
HSM, DNS Firewall, GSS-TSIG, Managing

176
00:13:59.999 --> 00:14:04.814
Microsoft Servers, DoT
and DoH Architecture,

177
00:14:04.814 --> 00:14:08.820
9.1.0 What's New; NIOS
9.1.0 blog (vendor).

178
00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:11.095
DHCP first.

179
00:14:11.095 --> 00:14:16.075
Failover peers form an association over
TCP, split clients by a hash of the

180
00:14:16.075 --> 00:14:21.382
MAC address, fifty-fifty by default, and
send each other lease updates directly.

181
00:14:21.382 --> 00:14:26.108
The Grid Master isn't involved in
failover, whatever older material says.

182
00:14:26.108 --> 00:14:32.507
An association serves ranges in one network
view only, and failover is IPv4 only.

183
00:14:32.507 --> 00:14:35.891
That last point matters
under the IPv6 mandate,

184
00:14:35.891 --> 00:14:39.708
though as we'll see in Module
2, Windows has the same gap.

185
00:14:39.708 --> 00:14:44.687
DHCPv6 itself is supported,
including prefix delegation,

186
00:14:44.687 --> 00:14:47.695
and fixed addresses arrived in 9.0.6.

187
00:14:47.695 --> 00:14:52.030
Fingerprinting matches options
55 and 60 and is on by default,

188
00:14:52.030 --> 00:14:55.770
and filters can deny leases
to device types per range.

189
00:14:55.770 --> 00:14:58.459
Lease history is archived and exportable,

190
00:14:58.459 --> 00:15:01.608
which is the address-to-asset
record NIST asks for.

191
00:15:01.608 --> 00:15:07.094
Dynamic DNS updates from DHCP are
something you turn on, not automatic.

192
00:15:07.094 --> 00:15:07.945
IPAM.

193
00:15:07.945 --> 00:15:12.395
A network view is a single routing
domain, up to one thousand per Grid.

194
00:15:12.395 --> 00:15:17.257
Views are mutually exclusive, so the same
address space can exist in two of them.

195
00:15:17.257 --> 00:15:20.982
A DHCP member serves
only one network view.

196
00:15:20.982 --> 00:15:23.562
Network containers hold the large blocks,

197
00:15:23.562 --> 00:15:28.795
and extensible attributes tag objects with
metadata such as division or cost center.

198
00:15:28.795 --> 00:15:32.366
Discovery uses ICMP, NetBIOS or TCP,

199
00:15:32.366 --> 00:15:37.908
and vDiscovery reads vCenter,
OpenStack, Azure and AWS.

200
00:15:37.908 --> 00:15:43.209
Network Insight, the layer-two and
layer-three device discovery, needs its

201
00:15:43.209 --> 00:15:48.683
own license and dedicated appliances that
run no DNS or DHCP; budget for that.

202
00:15:48.683 --> 00:15:52.657
Universal Asset Insights is
the SaaS inventory service.

203
00:15:52.657 --> 00:15:56.583
And AWS documents Infoblox
as the external authority

204
00:15:56.583 --> 00:16:00.246
for a VPC IPAM private
scope, on the Advanced tier.

205
00:16:00.246 --> 00:16:02.545
Two corrections to common claims.

206
00:16:02.545 --> 00:16:06.357
Separate network views permit
overlap; they don't prevent it.

207
00:16:06.357 --> 00:16:10.858
And the AWS IPAM integration
excludes the GovCloud regions.

208
00:16:10.858 --> 00:16:17.281
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x: DHCP
Failover, DHCPv6 RFC Compliance,

209
00:16:17.281 --> 00:16:25.886
DHCP Fingerprint Detection, Lease History,
Enabling DDNS, Configuring Network Views,

210
00:16:25.886 --> 00:16:32.310
About Discovery, About Network
Insight, Extensible Attributes;

211
00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:39.218
Universal Asset Insights; AWS VPC
IPAM user guide; AWS What's New, Nov.

212
00:16:39.218 --> 00:16:40.300
17, 2025.

213
00:16:41.050 --> 00:16:45.441
Infoblox Threat Defense,
formerly BloxOne Threat Defense,

214
00:16:45.441 --> 00:16:47.950
is the DNS-layer security product.

215
00:16:47.950 --> 00:16:52.506
It bundles three things: DNS Firewall
using response policy zones, TIDE,

216
00:16:52.506 --> 00:16:55.054
the Threat Intelligence Data Exchange,

217
00:16:55.054 --> 00:17:00.151
which aggregates and distributes
machine-readable intelligence, and Dossier,

218
00:17:00.151 --> 00:17:04.475
which researches an indicator across
about a dozen sources at once.

219
00:17:04.475 --> 00:17:07.906
It detects DNS tunneling
and data exfiltration,

220
00:17:07.906 --> 00:17:12.537
domain generation algorithms,
command-and-control and phishing.

221
00:17:12.537 --> 00:17:14.175
It's delivered two ways.

222
00:17:14.175 --> 00:17:17.397
From the cloud, through
the DNS Forwarding Proxy,

223
00:17:17.397 --> 00:17:20.700
the Infoblox Endpoint
agent and a cloud resolver.

224
00:17:20.700 --> 00:17:25.588
And on premises, as RPZ
feeds into NIOS or NIOS-X.

225
00:17:25.588 --> 00:17:29.268
On NIOS, Threat Insight
detects tunneling and adds

226
00:17:29.268 --> 00:17:33.037
the destination to a
mitigation RPZ automatically.

227
00:17:33.037 --> 00:17:38.793
Threat Defense Detection Mode streams DNS
reply metadata from NIOS to the cloud for

228
00:17:38.793 --> 00:17:43.150
passive detection; that metadata
includes client IP addresses,

229
00:17:43.150 --> 00:17:46.438
so treat it as a boundary
decision, not a checkbox.

230
00:17:46.438 --> 00:17:49.529
Licensing comes in four tiers: Essentials,

231
00:17:49.529 --> 00:17:53.238
Business On-Premises,
Business Cloud and Advanced.

232
00:17:53.238 --> 00:17:57.487
The cloud delivery pieces come
with Business Cloud and Advanced.

233
00:17:57.487 --> 00:18:00.750
Custom feed ingest in
TIDE is Advanced only.

234
00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:03.890
Dossier gives thirty-two
thousand queries a year on

235
00:18:03.890 --> 00:18:07.250
the Business tiers and
sixty-four thousand on Advanced.

236
00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:09.688
There's one strong independent signal.

237
00:18:09.688 --> 00:18:13.243
Google Cloud's own
documentation describes its

238
00:18:13.243 --> 00:18:16.975
DNS Armor threat detection
as powered by Infoblox.

239
00:18:16.975 --> 00:18:20.562
That's a cloud provider's
statement, not Infoblox marketing.

240
00:18:20.562 --> 00:18:21.937
And then there's the marketing.

241
00:18:21.937 --> 00:18:26.987
Infoblox says it stops ninety percent
of threats before the first query,

242
00:18:26.987 --> 00:18:30.690
detects threats sixty-eight
days before other tools,

243
00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:33.888
and has a false-positive
rate of 0.0002 percent.

244
00:18:33.888 --> 00:18:36.901
Nobody independent has
verified those figures.

245
00:18:36.901 --> 00:18:39.325
Keep them out of anything
an assessor reads.

246
00:18:39.325 --> 00:18:45.494
Sources: Infoblox About Threat
Defense; Licensing and Subscriptions;

247
00:18:45.494 --> 00:18:51.662
Dossier and TIDE Overview; About Threat
Insight (NIOS 9.1.x); TDDM for NIOS;

248
00:18:51.662 --> 00:18:58.720
Google Cloud DNS Armor docs; infoblox.com
Threat Defense page (vendor claims).

249
00:18:59.470 --> 00:19:02.358
Automation and logging,
the two things that decide

250
00:19:02.358 --> 00:19:04.970
whether the platform can
be governed at scale.

251
00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:09.408
On automation, Infoblox is
ahead of the other DDI vendors.

252
00:19:09.408 --> 00:19:16.669
NIOS's REST API, WAPI, shows version
2.14 in the 9.1.0 examples, and 9.1 adds

253
00:19:16.669 --> 00:19:24.320
OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect sign-in
for the API and immutable object IDs.

254
00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:29.258
Universal DDI has its own
REST API with API-key tokens.

255
00:19:29.258 --> 00:19:33.182
There's also a WAPI-compatible
API for Universal DDI,

256
00:19:33.182 --> 00:19:38.019
but Infoblox says it's for migration
only and isn't a long-term API.

257
00:19:38.019 --> 00:19:40.059
For infrastructure as code,

258
00:19:40.059 --> 00:19:45.557
the infobloxopen/infoblox Terraform
provider went to a 3.0 beta on October

259
00:19:45.557 --> 00:19:50.966
thirteenth: one provider for both NIOS
and Universal DDI, replacing three

260
00:19:50.966 --> 00:19:56.020
older ones, and not backward compatible,
so state has to be re-imported.

261
00:19:56.020 --> 00:20:00.095
Plan that migration; don't build
new work on the older providers.

262
00:20:00.095 --> 00:20:04.436
Ansible has current collections
for both, and ServiceNow publishes

263
00:20:04.436 --> 00:20:08.320
an Infoblox spoke that needs
IntegrationHub and a MID Server.

264
00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:10.008
Logging has sharper edges.

265
00:20:10.008 --> 00:20:14.722
Query and response capture rolls files
every ten minutes or hundred megabytes,

266
00:20:14.722 --> 00:20:18.436
with local retention capped by
model, and Infoblox recommends

267
00:20:18.436 --> 00:20:22.221
not logging queries and responses
together because of the load.

268
00:20:22.221 --> 00:20:25.834
dnstap runs on BIND in 9.1,
but drops logs rather than

269
00:20:25.834 --> 00:20:30.308
buffering them if the receiver is
down, so monitor the receiver.

270
00:20:30.308 --> 00:20:33.020
RPZ hits go to syslog in CEF.

271
00:20:33.020 --> 00:20:37.833
Cloud logs reach Splunk or Microsoft
Sentinel through the Data Connector;

272
00:20:37.833 --> 00:20:40.395
Splunk's own Infoblox add-on is archived.

273
00:20:40.395 --> 00:20:44.461
And the built-in Reporting
appliances have daily ingest caps:

274
00:20:44.461 --> 00:20:47.570
ten gigabytes on the
largest, five on the others.

275
00:20:47.570 --> 00:20:50.475
Exceed the cap five
times in thirty days and

276
00:20:50.475 --> 00:20:53.783
reporting locks until
Infoblox Support steps in.

277
00:20:53.783 --> 00:20:58.569
For federal retention, send logs to the
SIEM and size Reporting deliberately.

278
00:20:58.569 --> 00:21:03.933
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.0 What's
New; Universal DDI API Guide;

279
00:21:03.933 --> 00:21:11.013
WAPI 2.0 for Universal DDI; infobloxopen
terraform-provider-infoblox releases;

280
00:21:11.013 --> 00:21:17.449
infoblox-ansible and universal-ddi-ansible;
ServiceNow Infoblox spoke;

281
00:21:17.449 --> 00:21:21.632
DNS Query and Response
Captures; dnstap limits;

282
00:21:21.632 --> 00:21:27.900
Data Connector; Supported Reporting
Appliances; Splunkbase 2934.

283
00:21:28.650 --> 00:21:31.715
Module 2: why Infoblox
is a top recommendation,

284
00:21:31.715 --> 00:21:34.538
and why federal networks
need what it does.

285
00:21:34.538 --> 00:21:36.588
We'll build the case in four steps.

286
00:21:36.588 --> 00:21:40.036
First, map each federal
requirement to the capability

287
00:21:40.036 --> 00:21:43.562
it demands and to the Infoblox
feature that delivers it.

288
00:21:43.562 --> 00:21:46.516
Second, look closely at
what the FedRAMP listing

289
00:21:46.516 --> 00:21:49.838
covers and what stays inside
the agency's own boundary.

290
00:21:49.838 --> 00:21:54.509
Third, compare the assurance evidence
and the features against BlueCat,

291
00:21:54.509 --> 00:21:58.013
EfficientIP, Microsoft and
the native cloud services.

292
00:21:58.013 --> 00:22:00.394
And fourth, lay out the risks plainly,

293
00:22:00.394 --> 00:22:04.363
with how to manage each one and
what to ask for in the contract.

294
00:22:04.363 --> 00:22:09.400
A recommendation that hides its risks
isn't one an authorizing official can use.

295
00:22:09.400 --> 00:22:16.751
Sources: FedRAMP Marketplace
products.json; DISA STIG file store;

296
00:22:16.751 --> 00:22:22.160
NIST CMVP; NIAP PCL; CISA
CDM APL; NVD; CISA KEV.

297
00:22:22.910 --> 00:22:27.758
This is the core of the argument: each
requirement, the capability it demands,

298
00:22:27.758 --> 00:22:31.297
the Infoblox feature that
delivers it, and what to watch.

299
00:22:31.297 --> 00:22:35.971
Signing agency zones: the Grid
Master signs as a hidden primary,

300
00:22:35.971 --> 00:22:39.835
rolls keys automatically,
and can keep keys in an HSM.

301
00:22:39.835 --> 00:22:43.159
Watch the RSA-SHA1
default and the DS record

302
00:22:43.159 --> 00:22:46.298
you'll still hand to
the dot-gov registrar.

303
00:22:46.298 --> 00:22:50.969
Resilient, role-separated DNS:
Grid replication, HA pairs,

304
00:22:50.969 --> 00:22:56.335
a Grid Master Candidate, anycast,
and internal and external views.

305
00:22:56.335 --> 00:23:01.410
Watch that candidate promotion is manual,
so it belongs in a tested runbook.

306
00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:05.408
Encrypted DNS at agency
resolvers: from 9.1,

307
00:23:05.408 --> 00:23:09.748
BIND serves DoT and DoH,
but on X6 hardware only.

308
00:23:09.748 --> 00:23:12.758
Protective DNS: per-view forwarders send

309
00:23:12.758 --> 00:23:16.211
egress to CISA while the
Grid keeps local logs.

310
00:23:16.211 --> 00:23:17.260
Watch one thing.

311
00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:22.624
CISA's FAQ notes that some vendors don't
support the protocols for forwarding to its

312
00:23:22.624 --> 00:23:26.914
resolver, and our research found
no Infoblox document on encrypted

313
00:23:26.914 --> 00:23:30.822
forwarding to CISA, so confirm it
in a lab before you rely on it.

314
00:23:30.822 --> 00:23:33.843
Logging: query capture,
dnstap, lease history,

315
00:23:33.843 --> 00:23:38.414
CEF syslog and the Data Connector
cover what the Logging Reference

316
00:23:38.414 --> 00:23:42.985
Architecture maps; watch the ingest
caps and dnstap's drop behavior.

317
00:23:42.985 --> 00:23:48.238
Inventory for BOD 23-01, BOD
26-04 and CM-8: IPAM, discovery,

318
00:23:48.238 --> 00:23:55.391
cloud vDiscovery and Asset Insights
give the authoritative address record;

319
00:23:55.391 --> 00:24:00.085
watch the dedicated hardware
for Network Insight.

320
00:24:00.085 --> 00:24:09.585
IPv6: IPv6 Grids, DHCPv6 and prefix
delegation, but DHCP failover is IPv4 only.

321
00:24:09.585 --> 00:24:14.398
And validated cryptography is
the gap: Infoblox's FIPS 140-3

322
00:24:14.398 --> 00:24:19.310
modules are in NIST review, with
no active certificate today.

323
00:24:19.310 --> 00:24:22.805
Read across the rows:
seven of eight requirements

324
00:24:22.805 --> 00:24:25.448
land on one platform and one database.

325
00:24:25.448 --> 00:24:29.104
That consolidation is the case
for a governed DDI plane,

326
00:24:29.104 --> 00:24:31.886
and for Infoblox as a
strong way to build it.

327
00:24:31.886 --> 00:24:43.276
Sources: NIST SP 800-53r5; OMB
M-08-23, M-22-09, M-21-07; EO 14144;

328
00:24:43.276 --> 00:24:51.412
CISA Protective DNS FAQ;
CISA LRA; BOD 23-01, 26-04;

329
00:24:51.412 --> 00:25:03.580
Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x and Universal DDI
docs; NIST CMVP modules in process.

330
00:25:04.330 --> 00:25:08.388
FedRAMP is the most misquoted
part of any Infoblox proposal,

331
00:25:08.388 --> 00:25:09.930
so let's read the record.

332
00:25:09.930 --> 00:25:16.376
The Marketplace lists Infoblox
Government Cloud, package FR2017257053,

333
00:25:16.376 --> 00:25:22.928
as FedRAMP Certified at Moderate, which
the Marketplace now labels Class C,

334
00:25:22.928 --> 00:25:27.155
on the agency path, as SaaS
hosted in AWS GovCloud.

335
00:25:27.155 --> 00:25:32.993
It was certified in December 2022 through
the agency path, with Kratos as assessor.

336
00:25:32.993 --> 00:25:36.930
It shows one agency authorization
and a reuse count of zero.

337
00:25:36.930 --> 00:25:41.480
There's no High listing, and we
couldn't verify any DoD impact level.

338
00:25:41.480 --> 00:25:42.518
What's in scope?

339
00:25:42.518 --> 00:25:46.600
The listing names Universal
DDI, Universal Asset Insights,

340
00:25:46.600 --> 00:25:50.767
and the Threat Defense component
services TIDE and Dossier.

341
00:25:50.767 --> 00:25:56.001
Infoblox's July 2026 blog says the
offering now includes NIOS-X servers;

342
00:25:56.001 --> 00:25:59.904
treat that as a vendor claim
until the package says so.

343
00:25:59.904 --> 00:26:03.189
What isn't named: the Threat
Defense cloud resolver,

344
00:26:03.189 --> 00:26:06.317
the DNS Forwarding Proxy
and the Data Connector.

345
00:26:06.317 --> 00:26:11.293
Ask Infoblox for the authorization boundary
diagram before you design around them.

346
00:26:11.293 --> 00:26:14.876
And the biggest point: NIOS
appliances and vNIOS running

347
00:26:14.876 --> 00:26:18.306
on agency infrastructure
aren't a cloud service at all.

348
00:26:18.306 --> 00:26:20.630
No FedRAMP authorization covers them.

349
00:26:20.630 --> 00:26:25.597
They sit inside the agency's own
boundary and its own ATO, so a statement

350
00:26:25.597 --> 00:26:30.993
like "Infoblox's FedRAMP authorization
covers the DDI layer" is an overclaim.

351
00:26:30.993 --> 00:26:35.106
For High or DoD systems, the
path is agency-operated vNIOS,

352
00:26:35.106 --> 00:26:40.793
for example from the Azure Government
Marketplace, which Infoblox documents.

353
00:26:40.793 --> 00:26:42.255
Two practical notes.

354
00:26:42.255 --> 00:26:45.335
With reuse at zero,
expect to do your own ATO

355
00:26:45.335 --> 00:26:48.680
work rather than ride a
widely reused package.

356
00:26:48.680 --> 00:26:53.291
And Infoblox keeps separate FedRAMP
release notes whose latest entry is March

357
00:26:53.291 --> 00:26:57.203
fourteenth, while the commercial
notes run to October seventeenth,

358
00:26:57.203 --> 00:27:00.905
which suggests the FedRAMP
instance lags commercial features.

359
00:27:00.905 --> 00:27:02.505
Ask for the parity list.

360
00:27:02.505 --> 00:27:11.640
Sources: FedRAMP Marketplace
products.json, FR2017257053 (accessed Oct.

361
00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:16.817
22, 2026); Infoblox blog,
July 22, 2026 (vendor);

362
00:27:16.817 --> 00:27:24.020
What's New in Universal DDI
for FedRAMP; vNIOS for Azure.

363
00:27:24.770 --> 00:27:27.808
Assurance evidence, checked
on October twenty-second.

364
00:27:27.808 --> 00:27:29.882
DISA STIGs are a real strength.

365
00:27:29.882 --> 00:27:35.657
DISA's file store has an Infoblox
NIOS 9.x STIG, version 1 release 1,

366
00:27:35.657 --> 00:27:42.194
with a benchmark date of July 28, 2026
and forty-five rules, and an Infoblox

367
00:27:42.194 --> 00:27:48.732
8.x DNS STIG at version 1 release 4 from
September 30, with sixty-nine rules.

368
00:27:48.732 --> 00:27:52.120
The generic DNS SRG applies too.

369
00:27:52.120 --> 00:27:56.245
Neither BlueCat nor EfficientIP
has a vendor-specific STIG.

370
00:27:56.245 --> 00:28:00.207
FIPS 140 is the weak
spot, so say it plainly.

371
00:28:00.207 --> 00:28:04.345
Infoblox has no active FIPS
140 certificate today.

372
00:28:04.345 --> 00:28:11.302
Its FIPS 140-2 certificates, 4531 for
hardware and 4535 for virtual appliances,

373
00:28:11.302 --> 00:28:16.867
moved to the Historical list with
the September 21, 2026 sunset,

374
00:28:16.867 --> 00:28:23.182
and NIST says Historical modules may
be used for existing systems only.

375
00:28:23.182 --> 00:28:27.115
Four FIPS 140-3 submissions
are in the CMVP queue,

376
00:28:27.115 --> 00:28:32.620
in review or pending review since
late September and early October.

377
00:28:32.620 --> 00:28:36.341
Until those certificates post,
a new NIOS deployment that

378
00:28:36.341 --> 00:28:40.295
depends on validated crypto
can't cite an active certificate.

379
00:28:40.295 --> 00:28:43.895
NIAP: there's no current
Common Criteria certificate.

380
00:28:43.895 --> 00:28:50.120
NIOS 9.0 has been in evaluation
since August 17, 2026.

381
00:28:50.120 --> 00:28:53.335
Infoblox isn't on the CDM
Approved Products List,

382
00:28:53.335 --> 00:28:58.157
and that list isn't accepting new
submissions, so don't expect it to change.

383
00:28:58.157 --> 00:29:02.006
And a word on NIST SP 800-81 Revision 3,

384
00:29:02.006 --> 00:29:07.521
the Secure DNS Deployment
Guide, final in March 2026.

385
00:29:07.521 --> 00:29:11.833
It's the best practical guide
we have: Protective DNS,

386
00:29:11.833 --> 00:29:16.045
query logging, lease history,
encrypted DNS, DNSSEC.

387
00:29:16.045 --> 00:29:21.607
But two of its three authors, Cricket Liu
and Ross Gibson, are Infoblox employees.

388
00:29:21.607 --> 00:29:25.220
That's expertise and influence,
not an authorization.

389
00:29:25.220 --> 00:29:31.183
Whenever you cite 800-81r3 in support
of choosing Infoblox, disclose it.

390
00:29:31.183 --> 00:29:39.842
Sources: DISA STIG file store,
U_Infoblox_NIOS_9-x_V1R1 and U_IB_8-x_DNS_V1R4;

391
00:29:39.842 --> 00:29:48.502
NIST CMVP certificates 4531, 4535 and
modules-in-process list; NIAP PCL;

392
00:29:48.502 --> 00:29:58.120
Common Criteria Portal; CISA CDM APL,
April 2025; NIST SP 800-81r3 title page.

393
00:29:58.870 --> 00:30:01.245
A fair comparison, column by column.

394
00:30:01.245 --> 00:30:07.482
FedRAMP: Infoblox is the only dedicated DDI
vendor here with a listing, at Moderate.

395
00:30:07.482 --> 00:30:09.832
BlueCat and EfficientIP have none.

396
00:30:09.832 --> 00:30:13.929
But the hyperscalers' government
clouds are all FedRAMP High,

397
00:30:13.929 --> 00:30:16.420
a higher ceiling than Infoblox's SaaS.

398
00:30:16.420 --> 00:30:19.558
FIPS 140: BlueCat leads.

399
00:30:19.558 --> 00:30:27.283
It holds an active FIPS 140-3
certificate, number 5506, valid to 2029.

400
00:30:27.283 --> 00:30:31.307
Infoblox's is still in review,
and EfficientIP has none.

401
00:30:31.307 --> 00:30:35.338
STIGs: Infoblox has two
current vendor-specific STIGs;

402
00:30:35.338 --> 00:30:39.907
BlueCat and EfficientIP have
none, and Windows DNS has its own.

403
00:30:39.907 --> 00:30:44.000
Multi-cloud DNS: Infoblox
manages Route 53,

404
00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:48.794
Azure DNS and Google Cloud
DNS from one interface.

405
00:30:48.794 --> 00:30:53.145
BlueCat and EfficientIP claim similar
coverage on their product pages.

406
00:30:53.145 --> 00:30:57.895
Each cloud's native service covers only
that cloud, and Windows covers none.

407
00:30:57.895 --> 00:31:01.076
DNS security: Infoblox
has the broadest set,

408
00:31:01.076 --> 00:31:06.907
response policy zones plus TIDE intelligence
and tunneling and DGA analytics.

409
00:31:06.907 --> 00:31:10.116
Azure uses domain lists
and a Microsoft feed,

410
00:31:10.116 --> 00:31:15.751
AWS DNS Firewall Advanced adds DGA and
tunneling detection, Google's response

411
00:31:15.751 --> 00:31:21.646
policies are passthrough only, and Windows
DNS policies have no feed integration.

412
00:31:21.646 --> 00:31:25.060
DHCPv6 failover is missing
everywhere we checked,

413
00:31:25.060 --> 00:31:29.045
Infoblox and Windows included,
so it's not a differentiator.

414
00:31:29.045 --> 00:31:34.620
EfficientIP claims extra DHCP
high-availability modes; that's a vendor claim.

415
00:31:34.620 --> 00:31:38.035
Automation: two partner-tier
Terraform providers,

416
00:31:38.035 --> 00:31:42.833
two Ansible collections and ServiceNow
give Infoblox the deepest set.

417
00:31:42.833 --> 00:31:46.295
EfficientIP's Terraform
provider is community tier.

418
00:31:46.295 --> 00:31:51.357
Where alternatives win: BlueCat's
Micetro manages existing Windows and BIND

419
00:31:51.357 --> 00:31:56.182
servers in place, and a single-cloud
workload may need no third-party plane.

420
00:31:56.182 --> 00:31:58.731
So the fair claim is the one on the slide:

421
00:31:58.731 --> 00:32:03.432
a strong default for a hybrid federal
estate, not the only viable choice.

422
00:32:03.432 --> 00:32:09.480
Sources: FedRAMP Marketplace
products.json; NIST CMVP certificate 5506;

423
00:32:09.480 --> 00:32:13.915
DISA STIG store; BlueCat
Integrity and Micetro pages,

424
00:32:13.915 --> 00:32:17.645
EfficientIP SOLIDserver
datasheet (vendor);

425
00:32:17.645 --> 00:32:23.895
Microsoft Learn DNS Policies and DHCP
failover; Azure DNS resolver policy;

426
00:32:23.895 --> 00:32:30.346
Route 53 DNS Firewall Advanced; Google
Cloud response policies and DNS Armor;

427
00:32:30.346 --> 00:32:33.730
Terraform Registry; Ansible Galaxy.

428
00:32:34.480 --> 00:32:38.505
Now the risks, stated plainly,
with how to manage each one.

429
00:32:38.505 --> 00:32:40.943
First, cryptographic validation.

430
00:32:40.943 --> 00:32:44.305
There's no active FIPS
140 certificate today.

431
00:32:44.305 --> 00:32:49.949
The 140-2 certificates went Historical
with the September 21 sunset,

432
00:32:49.949 --> 00:32:53.217
and four 140-3 modules are in NIST review.

433
00:32:53.217 --> 00:32:55.393
BlueCat holds an active certificate.

434
00:32:55.393 --> 00:33:00.239
If your system requires validated
cryptography at award, make go-live depend

435
00:33:00.239 --> 00:33:04.404
on the issued certificate, or carry
the gap as a POA&M item with a date.

436
00:33:04.404 --> 00:33:08.020
Ask Infoblox which NIOS versions
the pending modules cover,

437
00:33:08.020 --> 00:33:11.418
because a certificate for the
wrong version doesn't help.

438
00:33:11.418 --> 00:33:17.692
Second, there's no current NIAP
certificate; NIOS 9.0 is in evaluation.

439
00:33:17.692 --> 00:33:21.187
And Infoblox isn't on the
CDM Approved Products List,

440
00:33:21.187 --> 00:33:23.543
which is closed to new submissions.

441
00:33:23.543 --> 00:33:28.442
Track the NIAP evaluation, and
don't promise anyone APL status.

442
00:33:28.442 --> 00:33:30.443
Third, vulnerabilities.

443
00:33:30.443 --> 00:33:36.983
NVD lists four NIOS CVEs from February
2025 rated between 9.1 and 9.8,

444
00:33:36.983 --> 00:33:44.355
including improper authentication for
Grids, and a remote-code-execution

445
00:33:44.355 --> 00:33:49.943
deserialization flaw,
CVE-2025-61880, from February 2026.

446
00:33:49.943 --> 00:33:55.281
None of the Infoblox CVEs is in CISA's
Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog.

447
00:33:55.281 --> 00:33:59.918
Write the patch cadence and the Long-Term
Support train into the ATO plan,

448
00:33:59.918 --> 00:34:03.705
and keep the Grid management
plane off untrusted networks.

449
00:34:03.705 --> 00:34:05.755
Fourth, the BIND dependency.

450
00:34:05.755 --> 00:34:09.891
NIOS and NIOS-X run on
BIND, and NIOS-X updates in

451
00:34:09.891 --> 00:34:14.680
September 2026 shipped to
address lists of BIND CVEs.

452
00:34:14.680 --> 00:34:23.405
On October eighth CISA added an old BIND
flaw, CVE-2015-5477, to the KEV catalog.

453
00:34:23.405 --> 00:34:28.623
Ask Infoblox for its BIND advisory SLA
and a written attestation that current

454
00:34:28.623 --> 00:34:31.835
releases aren't affected
by KEV-listed BIND CVEs,

455
00:34:31.835 --> 00:34:34.243
and track ISC's advisories yourself.

456
00:34:34.243 --> 00:34:38.217
Fifth, the SaaS ceiling is
Moderate, and reuse is zero.

457
00:34:38.217 --> 00:34:44.579
Use agency-operated vNIOS for High or
DoD systems, and budget a full ATO.

458
00:34:44.579 --> 00:34:53.540
Sources: NIST CMVP certificates 4531,
4535, 5506 and modules-in-process list;

459
00:34:53.540 --> 00:34:59.610
NIAP PCL; CISA CDM APL; NVD
CVE API (keyword Infoblox);

460
00:34:59.610 --> 00:35:09.650
CISA KEV catalog 2026.10.21; Infoblox
Cloud release notes; FedRAMP products.json.

461
00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:14.562
The second set of risks is about
cost, capacity and features.

462
00:35:14.562 --> 00:35:15.488
Licensing.

463
00:35:15.488 --> 00:35:19.308
Universal DDI uses three
token types, management,

464
00:35:19.308 --> 00:35:22.663
server and reporting,
sold in minimum packs.

465
00:35:22.663 --> 00:35:27.041
Tokens are a subscription entitlement
that expires at the end of the term,

466
00:35:27.041 --> 00:35:30.702
can only be rebalanced within a
type, and overage is handled by

467
00:35:30.702 --> 00:35:34.650
what Infoblox calls True Forward,
on a rolling three-month average.

468
00:35:34.650 --> 00:35:39.427
Model total cost over three to five
years, including the hardware refresh:

469
00:35:39.427 --> 00:35:42.637
Infoblox's end-of-life
policy typically supports

470
00:35:42.637 --> 00:35:45.613
hardware four years after
its last order date.

471
00:35:45.613 --> 00:35:47.038
Reporting capacity.

472
00:35:47.038 --> 00:35:51.663
We covered the ingest caps; five
overruns in thirty days locks reporting.

473
00:35:51.663 --> 00:35:54.751
Size the Reporting tier
for the retention you need,

474
00:35:54.751 --> 00:35:57.399
or make the SIEM the
system of record for logs.

475
00:35:57.399 --> 00:36:02.838
Encrypted DNS needs X6 hardware,
or an extra license on X5.

476
00:36:02.838 --> 00:36:07.050
Plan the refresh around
the encrypted DNS deadline.

477
00:36:07.050 --> 00:36:10.150
DNSSEC defaults to RSA with SHA-1.

478
00:36:10.150 --> 00:36:17.175
Put SHA-256 or ECDSA in the build standard
so nobody signs with the default.

479
00:36:17.175 --> 00:36:21.289
Hybrid limits: the Portal can't
promote a Grid Master Candidate or

480
00:36:21.289 --> 00:36:26.013
manage DHCP failover associations, so
keep Grid Manager runbooks for both.

481
00:36:26.013 --> 00:36:29.581
And stale collateral: an
older Infoblox blog post

482
00:36:29.581 --> 00:36:33.150
still says Universal DDI
isn't FedRAMP compliant.

483
00:36:33.150 --> 00:36:35.175
Cite only the Marketplace record.

484
00:36:35.175 --> 00:36:39.763
On the right are the procurement asks
that turn these risks into obligations.

485
00:36:39.763 --> 00:36:46.275
A FIPS 140-3 certificate timeline, the
versions it covers, and a remedy if it slips.

486
00:36:46.275 --> 00:36:50.500
A BIND advisory SLA with a
KEV not-affected attestation.

487
00:36:50.500 --> 00:36:54.062
The FedRAMP boundary diagram
and a commercial-parity list.

488
00:36:54.062 --> 00:36:56.576
Reporting sized for six months searchable.

489
00:36:56.576 --> 00:37:01.150
Caps on token true-forward, and a clause
guaranteeing you can export your data.

490
00:37:01.150 --> 00:37:07.453
Sources: Infoblox Universal DDI Tokens
FAQ and Supplemental Terms (vendor);

491
00:37:07.453 --> 00:37:14.366
Supported Reporting Appliances; NIOS 9.1.0
blog (vendor); Setting DNSSEC Parameters;

492
00:37:14.366 --> 00:37:18.635
Limitations of Universal
DDI; Infoblox EOL policy;

493
00:37:18.635 --> 00:37:22.960
Infoblox Top 12 Questions
post (search summary).

494
00:37:23.710 --> 00:37:26.735
Part 2: divisions under
central governance.

495
00:37:26.735 --> 00:37:29.960
Module 3 covers divisions on NIOS.

496
00:37:29.960 --> 00:37:32.085
Most agencies aren't one network.

497
00:37:32.085 --> 00:37:37.089
Bureaus, regional offices and labs run
their own address space and often their own

498
00:37:37.089 --> 00:37:41.505
DNS, and a central policy plane only
works if those divisions can still do

499
00:37:41.505 --> 00:37:45.773
their jobs through it, because a
division that can't will work around it.

500
00:37:45.773 --> 00:37:49.286
NIOS has a detailed permission
model for exactly this.

501
00:37:49.286 --> 00:37:54.410
We'll go through how it decides access, how
to scope an admin group to one division,

502
00:37:54.410 --> 00:37:59.691
the views and attributes that separate
divisions, approvals and their one big gap,

503
00:37:59.691 --> 00:38:04.273
sign-in, Microsoft servers, what stays
central, and a reporting surprise.

504
00:38:04.273 --> 00:38:13.740
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x and 9.0
administration guides; Book 08, Chapter 11.

505
00:38:14.490 --> 00:38:19.865
The NIOS permission model has three
levels: read-write, read-only and deny.

506
00:38:19.865 --> 00:38:23.996
Deny is the default for every resource,
which is the right starting point

507
00:38:23.996 --> 00:38:27.852
for divisions: a new group sees
nothing until you grant it something.

508
00:38:27.852 --> 00:38:29.777
Permissions come in two scopes.

509
00:38:29.777 --> 00:38:35.590
Global permissions cover every object of
a type, for example every DHCP network.

510
00:38:35.590 --> 00:38:40.766
Object permissions cover one specific
member, zone, response policy zone,

511
00:38:40.766 --> 00:38:45.178
network, record or fixed address,
together with what's inside it.

512
00:38:45.178 --> 00:38:47.665
Inheritance runs from parent to child,

513
00:38:47.665 --> 00:38:52.017
and you override it by setting a
permission at a more specific level,

514
00:38:52.017 --> 00:38:55.903
such as deny on one fixed address
inside a read-write network.

515
00:38:55.903 --> 00:39:00.202
When permissions overlap, NIOS
resolves them in a fixed order.

516
00:39:00.202 --> 00:39:03.366
It starts at the most
specific object and works up.

517
00:39:03.366 --> 00:39:07.404
At each level it checks permissions
assigned directly to the group first,

518
00:39:07.404 --> 00:39:10.053
then the group's roles in
the order they're listed.

519
00:39:10.053 --> 00:39:14.127
The first permission it finds wins,
and everything after it is ignored.

520
00:39:14.127 --> 00:39:17.861
So if one role grants read-only
on A records and a later role

521
00:39:17.861 --> 00:39:21.828
grants read-write on the same
records, the admin gets read-only.

522
00:39:21.828 --> 00:39:26.903
Grid Manager flags overlaps with a See
Conflicts link and an Overlaps filter.

523
00:39:26.903 --> 00:39:30.853
Roles are reusable permission
sets, up to twenty-one per group.

524
00:39:30.853 --> 00:39:36.048
Be careful with the system roles: DNS
Admin and DHCP Admin grant read-write

525
00:39:36.048 --> 00:39:41.840
across all members and all managed Microsoft
servers, far too broad for a division.

526
00:39:41.840 --> 00:39:43.952
The governance lever is on the left.

527
00:39:43.952 --> 00:39:47.345
Only superusers can create
admin groups and roles,

528
00:39:47.345 --> 00:39:52.191
or define extensible attributes, so a
division can't widen its own rights.

529
00:39:52.191 --> 00:39:55.152
And a local admin superuser always exists,

530
00:39:55.152 --> 00:39:59.215
so someone can still log in if
the directory or OCSP is down.

531
00:39:59.215 --> 00:40:04.403
Sources: Infoblox NIOS: About Administrative
Permissions, About Admin Groups,

532
00:40:04.403 --> 00:40:08.633
About Admin Roles, Applying
Permissions and Managing Overlaps,

533
00:40:08.633 --> 00:40:13.470
Creating Superuser Admin Groups,
Managing Extensible Attributes.

534
00:40:14.220 --> 00:40:18.189
Here is the recipe for scoping
one admin group to one division,

535
00:40:18.189 --> 00:40:20.757
built from Infoblox's permission pages.

536
00:40:20.757 --> 00:40:26.439
Step one, as a superuser, create a
limited-access group and turn on only

537
00:40:26.439 --> 00:40:32.020
the interfaces it needs: GUI, API,
or CLI with specific command groups.

538
00:40:32.020 --> 00:40:34.582
Limited-access groups
never get the console.

539
00:40:34.582 --> 00:40:39.405
For help-desk staff, a group can be
restricted to the Tasks Dashboard only,

540
00:40:39.405 --> 00:40:42.620
so they run approved task
packs and nothing else.

541
00:40:42.620 --> 00:40:45.170
Step two, grant object permissions.

542
00:40:45.170 --> 00:40:48.756
Read-write on the division's
network container or networks

543
00:40:48.756 --> 00:40:52.707
reaches every object inside them
and overrides global settings.

544
00:40:52.707 --> 00:40:57.464
If the division has overlapping space,
grant read-write on its own network

545
00:40:57.464 --> 00:41:01.908
view instead, which reaches its
networks, ranges and fixed addresses.

546
00:41:01.908 --> 00:41:07.217
For DNS, the documented pattern is read-only
on the parent DNS view and read-write

547
00:41:07.217 --> 00:41:12.607
on the division's zones, so the division
can create zones without editing the view.

548
00:41:12.607 --> 00:41:18.783
You can go finer, per record type:
read-write on MX and SRV, deny on CNAME.

549
00:41:18.783 --> 00:41:23.923
And Member DNS and Member DHCP permissions
confine the group's service work to its

550
00:41:23.923 --> 00:41:26.570
own members, with a separate permission to

551
00:41:26.570 --> 00:41:29.594
restart services without
changing properties.

552
00:41:29.594 --> 00:41:34.707
Only superusers can change
Grid-level DNS and DHCP properties.

553
00:41:34.707 --> 00:41:37.970
Step three, leave everything
else at the default deny.

554
00:41:37.970 --> 00:41:40.220
Step four is the one people miss.

555
00:41:40.220 --> 00:41:43.202
Turn on the Grid property
called Enable DNS

556
00:41:43.202 --> 00:41:46.183
Object Permissions in Networks and Ranges.

557
00:41:46.183 --> 00:41:50.861
With it on, creating an A, AAAA or
PTR record or a host needs read-write

558
00:41:50.861 --> 00:41:55.283
on both the zone and the network
or range that holds the address.

559
00:41:55.283 --> 00:41:58.212
Without it, a division with
write access to a shared

560
00:41:58.212 --> 00:42:01.420
zone could point a name at
another division's addresses.

561
00:42:01.420 --> 00:42:05.870
Fixed address and reservation permissions
are always enforced either way.

562
00:42:05.870 --> 00:42:10.375
Sources: Infoblox NIOS: Creating
Limited-Access Admin Groups;

563
00:42:10.375 --> 00:42:16.117
Defining Object Permissions; Administrative
Permissions for Network Views,

564
00:42:16.117 --> 00:42:21.681
IPAM Resources, DNS Views, Zones and
Resource Records; Permissions for DNS

565
00:42:21.681 --> 00:42:27.530
Resources in Networks and Ranges; DNS
and DHCP Permissions on Grid Members.

566
00:42:28.280 --> 00:42:31.097
Three constructs separate
divisions on NIOS,

567
00:42:31.097 --> 00:42:33.618
and each has a rule
that shapes the design.

568
00:42:33.618 --> 00:42:34.781
Network views.

569
00:42:34.781 --> 00:42:38.817
A network view is a single routing
domain with its own networks.

570
00:42:38.817 --> 00:42:43.267
Views are mutually exclusive, so two
of them can hold the same addresses;

571
00:42:43.267 --> 00:42:48.168
You can have up to a thousand, and the
default view can be renamed but not deleted.

572
00:42:48.168 --> 00:42:52.492
The design rule: a DHCP member
serves only one network view,

573
00:42:52.492 --> 00:42:56.006
and both failover peers
must serve the same one.

574
00:42:56.006 --> 00:43:00.480
So a division with overlapping
space needs its own DHCP members.

575
00:43:00.480 --> 00:43:05.640
Remember too that nothing on NIOS
enforces uniqueness across views;

576
00:43:05.640 --> 00:43:08.718
that's a Universal DDI
feature, in Module 4.

577
00:43:08.718 --> 00:43:12.452
DNS views give different
answers to different clients,

578
00:43:12.452 --> 00:43:16.592
matched on source address or TSIG
key, and the first match wins.

579
00:43:16.592 --> 00:43:22.295
Every network view gets an automatic DNS
view called default dot the view name,

580
00:43:22.295 --> 00:43:25.892
and a DNS view belongs to
exactly one network view.

581
00:43:25.892 --> 00:43:31.343
Only one DNS view per network view
can receive dynamic DNS updates.

582
00:43:31.343 --> 00:43:34.440
And order matters: put a
catch-all external view

583
00:43:34.440 --> 00:43:37.379
first and the internal
view is never reached.

584
00:43:37.379 --> 00:43:42.289
Extensible attributes are metadata:
such as Division and CostCenter,

585
00:43:42.289 --> 00:43:45.505
with types, allowed values
and a required flag.

586
00:43:45.505 --> 00:43:50.917
They inherit down from the network view to
the container, network, range and host.

587
00:43:50.917 --> 00:43:52.081
Two cautions.

588
00:43:52.081 --> 00:43:57.236
Required is enforced only in Grid
Manager; objects created through the API

589
00:43:57.236 --> 00:44:02.392
or CSV import can skip it, so your
automation has to enforce the Division tag.

590
00:44:02.392 --> 00:44:07.542
And attributes drive smart folders,
dashboards and reports, not permissions.

591
00:44:07.542 --> 00:44:10.767
We found no way to grant
access by attribute value.

592
00:44:10.767 --> 00:44:16.792
Access is always scoped by objects:
containers, networks, views and zones.

593
00:44:16.792 --> 00:44:23.235
Sources: Infoblox NIOS: Configuring
Network Views; About DNS Views; Managing

594
00:44:23.235 --> 00:44:30.640
Extensible Attributes; Configuring
Inheritable EAs; Smart Folders and EA filters.

595
00:44:31.390 --> 00:44:33.883
The approval workflow
is how central keeps a

596
00:44:33.883 --> 00:44:36.578
hand on division changes
without doing the work.

597
00:44:36.578 --> 00:44:39.039
Only superusers create workflows.

598
00:44:39.039 --> 00:44:43.338
Each workflow routes tasks from one
submitter group to an approver group; a

599
00:44:43.338 --> 00:44:48.190
submitter group has exactly one workflow,
while an approver group can serve several.

600
00:44:48.190 --> 00:44:52.840
Approvers approve, approve with
a new execution time, or reject.

601
00:44:52.840 --> 00:44:56.235
Approved tasks run with
the submitter's permissions

602
00:44:56.235 --> 00:44:58.627
and land in the audit log with a task ID.

603
00:44:58.627 --> 00:45:02.778
You can require a ticket number and
comments, and notify either side.

604
00:45:02.778 --> 00:45:08.411
What can go through approval: DNS zones,
views and records except SOA; hosts,

605
00:45:08.411 --> 00:45:13.306
networks, containers, ranges,
fixed addresses and reservations;

606
00:45:13.306 --> 00:45:19.216
Microsoft objects that NIOS supports;
response policy zones and their rules.

607
00:45:19.216 --> 00:45:22.140
Service restarts aren't
subject to approval.

608
00:45:22.140 --> 00:45:27.397
Some operations are blocked for submitters
even if they hold the permission:

609
00:45:27.397 --> 00:45:32.736
signing or unsigning zones, importing DS
records and rolling key-signing keys;

610
00:45:32.736 --> 00:45:35.776
reclaiming, resizing
or splitting networks;

611
00:45:35.776 --> 00:45:39.965
bulk edits and deletes; Recycle
Bin restores and RPZ ordering.

612
00:45:39.965 --> 00:45:43.603
Those stay with central staff,
which suits a federal model well.

613
00:45:43.603 --> 00:45:44.591
Now the gap.

614
00:45:44.591 --> 00:45:47.552
CSV imports bypass approval entirely.

615
00:45:47.552 --> 00:45:51.542
Infoblox's own page says CSV
imports do not require approvals,

616
00:45:51.542 --> 00:45:57.016
and Grid Manager shows a warning when a
submitter group holds CSV import permission.

617
00:45:57.016 --> 00:45:59.078
If you leave that permission in place,

618
00:45:59.078 --> 00:46:02.340
a division can load a thousand
records with no review at all.

619
00:46:02.340 --> 00:46:05.657
Remove CSV import from every
division submitter group,

620
00:46:05.657 --> 00:46:09.840
and give bulk loads to a central group
that works under a change ticket.

621
00:46:09.840 --> 00:46:15.858
Sources: Infoblox NIOS: Configuring Approval
Workflows; Creating Approval Workflows;

622
00:46:15.858 --> 00:46:18.700
Unsupported Operations for Submitters;

623
00:46:18.700 --> 00:46:24.080
Supported Tasks for Different Admin
Groups; Scheduling Tasks; Audit Log.

624
00:46:24.830 --> 00:46:27.462
Who can sign in, and
which group they land in,

625
00:46:27.462 --> 00:46:29.793
is the other half of division scoping.

626
00:46:29.793 --> 00:46:34.499
NIOS supports local accounts,
RADIUS, Active Directory,

627
00:46:34.499 --> 00:46:38.080
LDAP, TACACS+ and SAML,
in any combination.

628
00:46:38.080 --> 00:46:41.963
The authentication policy is an
ordered list of server groups,

629
00:46:41.963 --> 00:46:46.518
plus an ordered list that maps remote
groups to local admin groups by name.

630
00:46:46.518 --> 00:46:49.855
The first match wins, and there's
an optional default group.

631
00:46:49.855 --> 00:46:54.645
So a directory, for example an AD security
group per division, decides which

632
00:46:54.645 --> 00:47:00.130
division an administrator belongs to, and
AD nested groups are resolved recursively.

633
00:47:00.130 --> 00:47:03.880
For federal staff the important
one is certificate authentication.

634
00:47:03.880 --> 00:47:08.810
NIOS authenticates administrators
by X.509 client certificate,

635
00:47:08.810 --> 00:47:14.380
and its documentation names CAC and
PIV smart-card holders as the example.

636
00:47:14.380 --> 00:47:19.454
Certificate status is checked against
an ordered list of OCSP responders,

637
00:47:19.454 --> 00:47:23.792
and both the authentication policy
and the OCSP check must pass.

638
00:47:23.792 --> 00:47:28.631
The admin name is matched from the
certificate's UPN, email or subject.

639
00:47:28.631 --> 00:47:33.880
SAML 2.0 lists Azure SSO among
its identity provider types.

640
00:47:33.880 --> 00:47:38.343
The provider sends a group attribute,
and the user lands in the NIOS

641
00:47:38.343 --> 00:47:42.968
group whose name exactly matches;
otherwise in a default SAML group.

642
00:47:42.968 --> 00:47:44.131
Four things to check.

643
00:47:44.131 --> 00:47:48.854
Certificate-based sign-in to the API
doesn't work if the Grid Master holds a

644
00:47:48.854 --> 00:47:54.042
Cloud Network Automation or Cloud API
license, so decide that before you license.

645
00:47:54.042 --> 00:47:57.181
SAML-only users can't run CLI commands.

646
00:47:57.181 --> 00:48:02.047
Infoblox's Universal DDI guide
says Entra sends group object IDs,

647
00:48:02.047 --> 00:48:05.202
while NIOS needs an
exact group name match,

648
00:48:05.202 --> 00:48:10.430
and we couldn't confirm which the NIOS
Azure SSO type expects, so test it.

649
00:48:10.430 --> 00:48:12.738
And because the first mapping wins,

650
00:48:12.738 --> 00:48:16.717
order mappings so a user in two
groups lands where you intend.

651
00:48:16.717 --> 00:48:19.156
Keep the local admin as break-glass.

652
00:48:19.156 --> 00:48:23.318
Sources: Infoblox NIOS:
Authenticating Administrators;

653
00:48:23.318 --> 00:48:27.569
Defining the Authentication
Policy; About Remote Admins;

654
00:48:27.569 --> 00:48:33.680
Two-Factor and Certificate Authentication
Services; SAML pages; AD Nested Groups;

655
00:48:33.680 --> 00:48:39.260
Creating Limited-Access Admin Groups;
Universal DDI SAML for Entra ID.

656
00:48:40.010 --> 00:48:42.673
Three topics close the NIOS module.

657
00:48:42.673 --> 00:48:47.834
First, a division that still
runs Windows DNS and DHCP.

658
00:48:47.834 --> 00:48:53.302
NIOS manages those servers over MS-RPC
with no agent, read-only or read-write,

659
00:48:53.302 --> 00:48:57.710
with a Microsoft Management
license on the managing member.

660
00:48:57.710 --> 00:49:01.394
Each Windows server syncs
into exactly one network

661
00:49:01.394 --> 00:49:04.735
view and one DNS view,
fixed after the first sync.

662
00:49:04.735 --> 00:49:09.227
Each server object carries its own
permissions inside Grid Manager,

663
00:49:09.227 --> 00:49:13.719
so give the division's group rights
on its own servers rather than the

664
00:49:13.719 --> 00:49:18.448
system DNS Admin or DHCP Admin roles,
which cover every Microsoft server.

665
00:49:18.448 --> 00:49:23.143
And be careful: changing the managing
member, or switching from read-write

666
00:49:23.143 --> 00:49:27.609
to read-only, reverts any edits made
in Grid Manager since the last sync.

667
00:49:27.609 --> 00:49:30.635
Second, what stays
central in every design.

668
00:49:30.635 --> 00:49:35.700
The Grid Master and its candidates,
and Grid-level DNS and DHCP properties,

669
00:49:35.700 --> 00:49:38.523
which only superusers can change anyway.

670
00:49:38.523 --> 00:49:43.947
DNSSEC keys and signing, because only
the Grid Master or its HSM can sign.

671
00:49:43.947 --> 00:49:49.397
Response policy zones and Threat Defense
policy, so security posture is uniform.

672
00:49:49.397 --> 00:49:51.531
The definitions of groups, roles,

673
00:49:51.531 --> 00:49:55.323
attributes and workflows, and
the authentication policy.

674
00:49:55.323 --> 00:49:57.335
And the top-level address blocks.

675
00:49:57.335 --> 00:50:00.848
Third, a limitation that
surprises almost everyone.

676
00:50:00.848 --> 00:50:04.573
NIOS reporting doesn't filter
by object permissions.

677
00:50:04.573 --> 00:50:08.884
Infoblox's page says that when
you view a DNS or DHCP report,

678
00:50:08.884 --> 00:50:13.734
you see all the data, even for
objects you have no permission to see.

679
00:50:13.734 --> 00:50:18.995
So giving a division the Reporting app
exposes agency-wide DNS and DHCP data,

680
00:50:18.995 --> 00:50:22.835
which matters a great deal
for a law-enforcement bureau.

681
00:50:22.835 --> 00:50:27.085
Scope reporting by which dashboards
you share with each division's role,

682
00:50:27.085 --> 00:50:30.697
filtered by the Division attribute,
not by row-level security.

683
00:50:30.697 --> 00:50:35.717
Sources: Infoblox NIOS: Managing
Microsoft Servers; About Admin Roles;

684
00:50:35.717 --> 00:50:40.737
Administrative Permission for the
Grid; Configuring DNSSEC on a Grid;

685
00:50:40.737 --> 00:50:46.980
RPZ license and permissions; Reporting
permissions; EA filters and Smart Folders.

686
00:50:47.730 --> 00:50:52.655
Module 4: divisions on Universal
DDI, and the operating model.

687
00:50:52.655 --> 00:50:57.086
Universal DDI has its own
divisional construct, access views,

688
00:50:57.086 --> 00:51:03.167
and its own answer to overlapping address
space, IPAM Federation, which NIOS lacks.

689
00:51:03.167 --> 00:51:06.653
We'll cover those and their
limits, including a conflict in

690
00:51:06.653 --> 00:51:10.356
Infoblox's own documentation
you'll need to resolve with them.

691
00:51:10.356 --> 00:51:14.265
Then we'll step back to four delegation
patterns and their trade-offs,

692
00:51:14.265 --> 00:51:18.656
walk through an illustrative design for
four divisions, set out who does what,

693
00:51:18.656 --> 00:51:22.155
and end with a rollout sequence
and the decisions to take next.

694
00:51:22.155 --> 00:51:28.062
Sources: Infoblox Portal and
Universal DDI documentation;

695
00:51:28.062 --> 00:51:30.960
Book 08, Chapters 09 and 10.

696
00:51:31.710 --> 00:51:36.913
On Universal DDI the division construct
is the access view, which Infoblox

697
00:51:36.913 --> 00:51:42.535
describes as a logical container for DDI
resources, built for divisional admins.

698
00:51:42.535 --> 00:51:45.435
The account itself is
the default access view.

699
00:51:45.435 --> 00:51:49.898
Corporate admins create sub-views under
it, and only one level is allowed.

700
00:51:49.898 --> 00:51:54.223
Divisional admins can't see or manage
anything outside their sub-view.

701
00:51:54.223 --> 00:51:58.523
You can assign IP spaces, address
blocks, DNS views and zones,

702
00:51:58.523 --> 00:52:02.297
including synced cloud and
Active Directory zones.

703
00:52:02.297 --> 00:52:05.747
Assign an IP space and
everything in it follows.

704
00:52:05.747 --> 00:52:07.610
The constraints are significant.

705
00:52:07.610 --> 00:52:11.636
Users, roles and policies are
defined only at the default view,

706
00:52:11.636 --> 00:52:14.635
so divisions can't
administer their own users.

707
00:52:14.635 --> 00:52:19.585
Access views can't be deleted or renamed
once created, so name them carefully.

708
00:52:19.585 --> 00:52:21.873
They take about ten minutes to propagate.

709
00:52:21.873 --> 00:52:25.235
And only interactive
users work fully in them;

710
00:52:25.235 --> 00:52:28.510
service users with API
keys can't log in to one.

711
00:52:28.510 --> 00:52:34.273
IP spaces are the Universal DDI version
of network views, and they can overlap.

712
00:52:34.273 --> 00:52:40.582
What NIOS lacks is IPAM Federation, which
enforces uniqueness across Universal DDI,

713
00:52:40.582 --> 00:52:45.314
NIOS and cloud IPAM, with realms,
federated blocks, delegations,

714
00:52:45.314 --> 00:52:49.784
overlapping blocks for space
behind NAT, and reserved blocks.

715
00:52:49.784 --> 00:52:54.060
Access policies bind a role, a
user group and an access view.

716
00:52:54.060 --> 00:52:58.535
Keep the Threat Defense and access-control
administrator policies central.

717
00:52:58.535 --> 00:53:02.443
Entra sign-in uses a
non-gallery SAML app whose group

718
00:53:02.443 --> 00:53:06.085
claim carries object IDs,
mapped to Portal groups.

719
00:53:06.085 --> 00:53:08.498
The audit log keeps thirteen months.

720
00:53:08.498 --> 00:53:10.460
And a documentation conflict.

721
00:53:10.460 --> 00:53:15.337
One Infoblox page lists IPAM, DNS
and DHCP roles for access views;

722
00:53:15.337 --> 00:53:19.860
another says only IPAM roles
are supported in this release.

723
00:53:19.860 --> 00:53:24.960
Confirm with Infoblox before you
design around DNS or DHCP roles.

724
00:53:24.960 --> 00:53:29.940
Sources: Infoblox Portal: About
Access Views, Supported Objects,

725
00:53:29.940 --> 00:53:36.236
Setting Up Access Control, Creating Access
Views, Interactive and Service Users,

726
00:53:36.236 --> 00:53:40.934
Access Policies, Restricting
User Groups, SAML for Entra ID,

727
00:53:40.934 --> 00:53:47.130
Mapping User Groups, Audit Logs;
Universal DDI: IP Spaces, IPAM Federation.

728
00:53:47.880 --> 00:53:51.643
Put together, the constructs
give four delegation patterns.

729
00:53:51.643 --> 00:53:55.403
Pattern (a): one central Grid,
with division admin groups scoped

730
00:53:55.403 --> 00:53:59.755
by object permissions and changes
routed through approval workflows.

731
00:53:59.755 --> 00:54:03.907
Its risks are permission sprawl,
first-match ordering surprises,

732
00:54:03.907 --> 00:54:07.905
the CSV import bypass, and
reporting that ignores permissions.

733
00:54:07.905 --> 00:54:10.443
Central keeps the definitions of groups,

734
00:54:10.443 --> 00:54:13.280
roles and attributes,
and the Grid properties.

735
00:54:13.280 --> 00:54:17.744
Pattern (b): the division owns
members inside the central Grid,

736
00:54:17.744 --> 00:54:21.405
with member DNS and DHCP
and restart permissions.

737
00:54:21.405 --> 00:54:26.493
It gets local resilience and local
restarts, confined to its own appliances.

738
00:54:26.493 --> 00:54:30.238
But it can't change Grid-level
properties, and if a zone is signed,

739
00:54:30.238 --> 00:54:32.831
the Grid Master is still
its hidden primary.

740
00:54:32.831 --> 00:54:37.343
Central keeps the Grid Master, its
candidates and the DNSSEC keys.

741
00:54:37.343 --> 00:54:41.844
Pattern (c): the division gets
its own network view on NIOS,

742
00:54:41.844 --> 00:54:44.681
or its own IP space on Universal DDI.

743
00:54:44.681 --> 00:54:48.465
It's the only way to hold
overlapping private address space,

744
00:54:48.465 --> 00:54:51.092
and it draws a clean visibility boundary.

745
00:54:51.092 --> 00:54:56.617
It costs dedicated DHCP members per
view, and on NIOS nothing enforces

746
00:54:56.617 --> 00:55:01.855
uniqueness across views; on
Universal DDI, IPAM Federation does.

747
00:55:01.855 --> 00:55:04.167
Central keeps the top-level blocks.

748
00:55:04.167 --> 00:55:10.168
Pattern (d): delegate a zone to DNS
servers the division runs, Infoblox or not.

749
00:55:10.168 --> 00:55:15.651
Full autonomy, at a price: central loses
the audit trail, RPZ coverage and DNSSEC

750
00:55:15.651 --> 00:55:21.218
for that child zone, and the delegation
must sit inside a zone with a Grid primary.

751
00:55:21.218 --> 00:55:26.205
Central keeps the parent, the NS and
glue records, and the DS records.

752
00:55:26.205 --> 00:55:32.555
Most agencies mix them: (a) as the default,
(b) where local resilience matters,

753
00:55:32.555 --> 00:55:38.693
(c) only for overlap, and (d) only where
autonomy is worth the lost coverage.

754
00:55:38.693 --> 00:55:45.477
Sources: Synthesis of Infoblox NIOS:
Administrative Permissions, Managing Overlaps,

755
00:55:45.477 --> 00:55:51.211
DNS and DHCP Permissions on Grid
Members, Network Views, Delegated Zones,

756
00:55:51.211 --> 00:55:55.990
DNSSEC on a Grid, Approval
Workflows, Reporting permissions;

757
00:55:55.990 --> 00:56:00.080
Universal DDI: Access
Views, IPAM Federation.

758
00:56:00.830 --> 00:56:02.217
Here's a worked example.

759
00:56:02.217 --> 00:56:05.893
It's an illustrative design,
not a validated configuration.

760
00:56:05.893 --> 00:56:11.067
The agency owns ten slash eight and
agency dot gov, with four divisions.

761
00:56:11.067 --> 00:56:13.942
HQ is the central network operations team.

762
00:56:13.942 --> 00:56:19.280
LEB is a law-enforcement bureau that needs
confidentiality from the other divisions.

763
00:56:19.280 --> 00:56:24.442
REG is a regional office still
running Windows DNS and DHCP.

764
00:56:24.442 --> 00:56:30.068
And LAB is a research lab whose ten
dot zero slash sixteen overlaps HQ.

765
00:56:30.068 --> 00:56:34.840
HQ, LEB and REG share the network
view called agency, each with its own

766
00:56:34.840 --> 00:56:40.293
slash twelve container tagged with a
Division attribute that inherits down.

767
00:56:40.293 --> 00:56:44.216
HQ's group gets read-write on
its container and zones and

768
00:56:44.216 --> 00:56:48.906
read-only on the internal view;
central keeps agency dot gov itself.

769
00:56:48.906 --> 00:56:53.439
LEB adds pattern (b): its zone
is served by its own members,

770
00:56:53.439 --> 00:56:57.368
with member DNS, DHCP and
restart rights on them.

771
00:56:57.368 --> 00:57:01.673
REG's Windows servers are managed from
the Grid, and its group gets rights

772
00:57:01.673 --> 00:57:05.692
on those server objects and its
zone, not the system Microsoft roles.

773
00:57:05.692 --> 00:57:07.592
LAB is the overlap case.

774
00:57:07.592 --> 00:57:10.379
It gets its own network view, nv-lab,

775
00:57:10.379 --> 00:57:15.555
with its own DHCP members, because
a DHCP member serves one view.

776
00:57:15.555 --> 00:57:19.376
And its zone is delegated to
two lab-run BIND servers,

777
00:57:19.376 --> 00:57:22.931
pattern (d), inside the
Grid-primary agency dot gov.

778
00:57:22.931 --> 00:57:27.730
If agency dot gov is signed, the
lab hands its DS records to central.

779
00:57:27.730 --> 00:57:32.246
Grid-wide controls: turn
on DNS object permissions,

780
00:57:32.246 --> 00:57:35.580
so HQ can't point a name into LEB's space.

781
00:57:35.580 --> 00:57:38.493
No CSV import for any division group.

782
00:57:38.493 --> 00:57:40.710
One approval workflow per division,

783
00:57:40.710 --> 00:57:44.256
approved by a central group,
with a ticket number required.

784
00:57:44.256 --> 00:57:49.193
SAML group names that match exactly,
and the local admin as break-glass.

785
00:57:49.193 --> 00:57:52.759
For LEB, keep its data out of
other divisions' dashboards,

786
00:57:52.759 --> 00:57:57.954
and consider a bureau-lead approver group
if central shouldn't see change detail.

787
00:57:57.954 --> 00:58:03.653
Sources: Illustrative design synthesized
from Infoblox NIOS: Network Views,

788
00:58:03.653 --> 00:58:07.659
DNS Views, Extensible
Attributes, Object Permissions,

789
00:58:07.659 --> 00:58:13.179
DNS Resources in Networks and Ranges,
Delegated Zones, Approval Workflows,

790
00:58:13.179 --> 00:58:17.900
SAML, Managing Microsoft
Servers, Reporting permissions.

791
00:58:18.650 --> 00:58:19.600
Who does what.

792
00:58:19.600 --> 00:58:25.101
This matrix is adapted from Book 08's
responsibility matrix, which separates

793
00:58:25.101 --> 00:58:30.688
deciding from doing: D decides and is
accountable, R does the work, C is consulted.

794
00:58:30.688 --> 00:58:33.638
I've added a Division column
for the delegated model.

795
00:58:33.638 --> 00:58:38.778
The CIO decides the platform and deployment
model, NIOS, the SaaS, or hybrid,

796
00:58:38.778 --> 00:58:44.087
with the central DDI team doing the
evaluation and everyone else consulted.

797
00:58:44.087 --> 00:58:47.436
The authorizing official decides
whether a cloud-delivered

798
00:58:47.436 --> 00:58:50.588
management plane is authorized
at the right impact level.

799
00:58:50.588 --> 00:58:55.336
The CIO also decides how divisions
are scoped in the policy plane,

800
00:58:55.336 --> 00:59:00.788
and the central DDI team builds it: admin
groups, network views, access views.

801
00:59:00.788 --> 00:59:05.551
Divisions are consulted, because a scope
they can't work in becomes a workaround.

802
00:59:05.551 --> 00:59:08.664
Central DDI owns the
top-level address blocks

803
00:59:08.664 --> 00:59:11.450
and namespace design, deciding and doing.

804
00:59:11.450 --> 00:59:16.642
DNSSEC signing and key rollover, and
response policy and Threat Defense policy,

805
00:59:16.642 --> 00:59:21.677
are control requirements, so the CISO
decides them and central DDI runs them,

806
00:59:21.677 --> 00:59:25.375
with security operations
consulted on response policy.

807
00:59:25.375 --> 00:59:29.850
Standard record changes in a division's
zone are where delegation pays off.

808
00:59:29.850 --> 00:59:34.739
Central decided in advance what a standard
change is; the division does the work,

809
00:59:34.739 --> 00:59:38.813
inside its scope, through the
approval workflow where one applies.

810
00:59:38.813 --> 00:59:43.969
High-impact changes, such as zones,
NS and SRV records, apex records,

811
00:59:43.969 --> 00:59:50.176
wildcards and RPZ, stay with central to
decide and do, with divisions consulted.

812
00:59:50.176 --> 00:59:53.552
And an encrypted DNS exception
that has no end date,

813
00:59:53.552 --> 00:59:58.056
or has outlived the agency's limit,
goes to the authorizing official,

814
00:59:58.056 --> 01:00:02.800
because at that point it's a risk
acceptance, not a configuration choice.

815
01:00:02.800 --> 01:00:05.030
Sources: Book 08, Chapter 09,

816
01:00:05.030 --> 01:00:11.517
"Decision rights and work are different
things," "The roles," "The responsibility

817
01:00:11.517 --> 01:00:18.060
matrix"; Infoblox NIOS approval
workflows; Universal DDI access policies.

818
01:00:18.810 --> 01:00:23.710
To close, a rollout sequence, the
decisions to take, and the next steps.

819
01:00:23.710 --> 01:00:28.872
The sequence follows Book 08's
canonical order, adapted for divisions.

820
01:00:28.872 --> 01:00:34.097
One: inventory everything first,
zones, scopes, forwarders, and every

821
01:00:34.097 --> 01:00:39.973
record Active Directory DNS holds,
before any domain controller is touched.

822
01:00:39.973 --> 01:00:44.184
Two: build the central pieces,
the Grid Master and candidates,

823
01:00:44.184 --> 01:00:47.553
the HSM if you'll use one,
and a build standard that

824
01:00:47.553 --> 01:00:51.259
sets the DNSSEC algorithm,
logging and STIG settings.

825
01:00:51.259 --> 01:00:55.683
Three: coexist, by managing the
existing Windows DNS and DHCP

826
01:00:55.683 --> 01:01:00.460
servers from the Grid in place, so
nothing moves until you're ready.

827
01:01:00.460 --> 01:01:04.531
Four: onboard divisions one at a
time, starting with pattern (a),

828
01:01:04.531 --> 01:01:09.823
the simplest, and adding views or
delegations only where a division needs them.

829
01:01:09.823 --> 01:01:12.059
Five: federate the clouds.

830
01:01:12.059 --> 01:01:16.490
Six: harden and evidence, with the
STIG applied, logs at retention,

831
01:01:16.490 --> 01:01:20.085
and audit trails the
authorizing official can read.

832
01:01:20.085 --> 01:01:21.760
The decisions to take now.

833
01:01:21.760 --> 01:01:27.948
Which platform model, NIOS, Universal
DDI or hybrid, for each impact level.

834
01:01:27.948 --> 01:01:30.462
Which delegation pattern
for each division,

835
01:01:30.462 --> 01:01:34.198
and who approves changes for a
bureau that needs confidentiality.

836
01:01:34.198 --> 01:01:39.948
Whether to wait for the FIPS 140-3
certificate or proceed with a POA&M.

837
01:01:39.948 --> 01:01:43.248
And where logs will live to
stay searchable for six months.

838
01:01:43.248 --> 01:01:44.497
And the next steps.

839
01:01:44.497 --> 01:01:47.910
Send Infoblox the procurement
asks from Module 2.

840
01:01:47.910 --> 01:01:50.379
Confirm which roles access views support,

841
01:01:50.379 --> 01:01:53.060
and what value Entra
sends for group matching.

842
01:01:53.060 --> 01:01:55.909
Lab-test encrypted forwarding to CISA's

843
01:01:55.909 --> 01:01:59.260
Protective DNS and the
DNSSEC algorithm change.

844
01:01:59.260 --> 01:02:01.523
And draft each division's scope and the

845
01:02:01.523 --> 01:02:04.422
responsibility matrix
with the division itself.

846
01:02:04.422 --> 01:02:09.803
In the program's analysis, Infoblox is one
of the strongest ways to build a governed

847
01:02:09.803 --> 01:02:14.472
DDI plane for a federal agency, as long
as you manage its risks in the open.

848
01:02:14.472 --> 01:02:18.618
One note on standing: this is
the program's research analysis,

849
01:02:18.618 --> 01:02:22.348
under review, not an endorsement
by any federal agency.

850
01:02:22.348 --> 01:02:23.110
Thank you.

851
01:02:23.110 --> 01:02:27.758
Sources: Book 08, index (canonical
sequence) and Chapter 10;

852
01:02:27.758 --> 01:02:31.713
Infoblox NIOS and Universal
DDI documentation;

853
01:02:31.713 --> 01:02:37.600
federal requirements and validation
research cited in Modules 1 and 2.
